    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/alexander-nevsky"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/alexander-nevsky.jpg" alt="Alexander Nevsky" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/alexander-nevsky">Alexander Nevsky</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:AlexanderNevskyTitul.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Collective of Kremlin Armory artists, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>, a small fortified settlement on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moskva-river">Moskva River</a> first mentioned in the chronicles under 1147, lay on the western edge of the Rus' principality of Vladimir-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suzdal">Suzdal</a>, which had submitted to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongols">Mongols</a> after their invasion of 1237–1240. On the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-nevsky">Alexander Nevsky</a> in 1263 the town passed, according to tradition, to his youngest son Daniel, then a small child, and under him it became the seat of a separate line of princes. Daniel enlarged his modest <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/appanage">appanage</a> by taking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kolomna">Kolomna</a> from the Principality of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ryazan">Ryazan</a> around 1301 and inheriting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pereslavl-zalessky">Pereslavl-Zalessky</a> in 1302. His son Yury married a sister of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzbek-khan">Uzbek Khan</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-horde">Golden Horde</a> and obtained the khan's patent (<em>yarlyk</em>) for the grand principality of Vladimir in 1317, beginning a bitter rivalry with the princes of Tver. When Tver rose against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tatars">Tatars</a> in 1327, Yury's brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivan-i-of-moscow">Ivan I Kalita</a> joined the Horde's punitive expedition and was rewarded with the grand princely title, which he held alone from 1331. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peter-of-moscow">Metropolitan Peter</a>, head of the Rus' church, settled in Moscow around 1325 and died there in 1326, and his successor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theognostus-metropolitan-of-kiev">Theognostus</a> made the town his residence, giving the young dynasty a religious centre to add to the lucrative right, which came with the grand princely title, to collect the Horde's tribute from the other Rus' princes.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/moscow"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/moscow.jpg" alt="Moscow" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Saint_Basil%27s_Cathedral_and_the_Red_Square.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">U.S. Department of State, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Muscovite society was overwhelmingly rural. Peasants grew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rye">rye</a>, oats and barley on the poor soils of the forest zone, supplementing farming with fishing, hunting, beekeeping and the trapping of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fur-trade">furs</a>. Most peasants were still free to move, although the law code of 1497 restricted their right to leave a landlord to the weeks around St George's Day in late November. Above them stood the grand prince, his kin and a limited circle of boyar families, served by lesser servitors, while monasteries became great landowners: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trinity-lavra-of-st-sergius">Trinity Monastery</a> founded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sergius-of-radonezh">Sergius of Radonezh</a> around the 1340s inspired disciples who founded new monasteries across the northern forests and helped to colonise them. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> itself grew into a large town of wooden houses around the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow-kremlin">Kremlin</a>, home to craftsmen, icon painters and merchants. Furs, wax, honey and leather were the main exports, traded through Novgorod to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hanseatic-league">Hanseatic League</a>, down the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volga">Volga</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kazan">Kazan</a> and the steppe, and south towards the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crimea">Crimea</a>, where Moscow's merchants known as <em>surozhane</em> dealt in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silk">silks</a> and other luxuries. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox Church</a> shaped daily life and culture, and the icons of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andrei-rublev">Andrei Rublev</a>, above all his <em>Trinity</em> of the early fifteenth century, mark the high point of Muscovite religious art.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-kulikovo"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-kulikovo.jpg" alt="Battle of Kulikovo" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-kulikovo">Battle of Kulikovo</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dmitry_Donskoy_in_the_Battle_of_Kulikovo.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Adolphe Yvon, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Muscovy's armies were built around the cavalry of the grand prince's court and the retinues of boyars and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/appanage">appanage</a> princes, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mounted-archer">mounted archers</a> who fought much like their steppe opponents. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dmitry-donskoy">Dmitry Donskoy</a> replaced the oak walls of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow-kremlin">Kremlin</a> with white limestone in 1367–1368, and the new fortress withstood sieges by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/algirdas">Algirdas</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a> in 1368 and 1370. On 8 September 1380 Dmitry led a coalition of Rus' princes to victory over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tatars">Tatar</a> commander <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamai">Mamai</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-kulikovo">Battle of Kulikovo</a> on the upper Don, but two years later Khan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokhtamysh">Tokhtamysh</a> took and burned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>, and in 1408 the emir <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edigu">Edigu</a> besieged it again. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivan-iii">Ivan III</a> the army grew larger and better organised. His forces crushed Novgorod at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-shelon">Battle of the Shelon River</a> in 1471, and in 1480 he faced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akhmat-khan">Akhmat Khan</a> of the Great Horde across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ugra-river">Ugra River</a> until the Tatars withdrew without a battle, an episode remembered as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-stand-on-the-ugra-river">Great Stand on the Ugra River</a>. Wars with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-duchy-of-lithuania">Grand Duchy of Lithuania</a> in 1487–1494 and 1500–1503, including the victory at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-vedrosha">Battle of Vedrosha</a> in 1500, won the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chernigov">Chernigov</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/severia">Seversk lands</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vasily-iii-of-moscow">Vasily III</a> captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/smolensk">Smolensk</a> in 1514, although a Polish-Lithuanian army defeated his forces at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-orsha">Battle of Orsha</a> the same year. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/field-artillery">Cannon</a> and Italian-designed brick fortifications appeared from the 1470s.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/sudebnik-of-1497"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sudebnik-of-1497.jpg" alt="Sudebnik of 1497" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/sudebnik-of-1497">Sudebnik of 1497</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sudebnik_of_1497.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">anonimus, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Muscovite government grew out of the grand prince's household. Land and authority were divided by testament among the ruler's sons as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/appanage">appanages</a>, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>'s princes increasingly favoured the eldest son, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dmitry-donskoy">Dmitry Donskoy</a>'s will of 1389 treated the grand principality of Vladimir as hereditary property for the first time. The principle was tested in the civil war of 1425–1453, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vasily-ii-of-moscow">Vasily II</a> fought his uncle <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yury-of-zvenigorod">Yury of Zvenigorod</a> and his cousins <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vasily-kosoy">Vasily Kosoy</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dmitry-shemyaka">Dmitry Shemyaka</a>; Vasily was captured by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tatars">Tatars</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ulugh-muhammad">Ulugh Muhammad</a> in 1445 and blinded by his rivals in 1446, yet regained the throne with the backing of the church and the boyars, and his victory ended claims by collateral lines. A council of boyars advised the ruler, governors (<em>namestniki</em>) ran districts and lived off fees known as <em>kormlenie</em>, or 'feeding', and from the late fifteenth century state secretaries (<em>dyaki</em>) kept written records. After the annexation of Novgorod, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivan-iii">Ivan III</a> confiscated estates and granted them as <em>pomestie</em>, land held in return for military service, creating a cavalry <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gentry">gentry</a> dependent on the crown. His law code, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sudebnik-of-1497">Sudebnik of 1497</a>, standardised court procedure. Ivan's marriage to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sophia-palaiologina">Sophia Palaiologina</a> in 1472, his use of the double-headed eagle and the title 'sovereign of all Rus'' expressed a growing claim to autocratic, quasi-imperial authority.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/dormition-cathedral-moscow"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dormition-cathedral-moscow.jpg" alt="Dormition Cathedral, Moscow" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/dormition-cathedral-moscow">Dormition Cathedral, Moscow</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dormition_Cathedral,_Moscow.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Daniel Kruczynski, CC BY-SA 2.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Muscovy turned a minor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/appanage">appanage</a> into the largest state in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> and fixed the political and religious pattern of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> for centuries. Its princes 'gathered the Russian lands' by purchase, inheritance, marriage, diplomacy and war, absorbing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nizhny-novgorod">Nizhny Novgorod</a> in 1392, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yaroslavl">Yaroslavl</a> in 1463, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rostov">Rostov</a> in 1474, the Novgorod Republic in 1478, Tver in 1485, Vyatka in 1489, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pskov">Pskov</a> in 1510 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ryazan">Ryazan</a> around 1521. The fall of the city republics ended the alternative tradition of assemblies (<em>veche</em>) that Novgorod and Pskov had represented. In church affairs, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> rejected the union with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> agreed at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/council-of-florence">Council of Florence</a> in 1439, and in 1448 Russian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> chose <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jonah-metropolitan-of-moscow">Jonah</a> as metropolitan without approval from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, a de facto independence confirmed after the Ottoman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fall-of-constantinople">conquest of Constantinople</a> in 1453. Writers such as the monk <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philotheus-of-pskov">Philotheus of Pskov</a> later presented Moscow as the '<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/third-rome">Third Rome</a>', heir of a fallen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantium</a>, an idea that shaped Russian self-understanding. Italian architects, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aristotele-fioravanti">Aristotele Fioravanti</a>, who built the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dormition-cathedral-moscow">Cathedral of the Dormition</a> in 1475–1479, gave the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow-kremlin">Moscow Kremlin</a> much of its present form. Muscovy also absorbed steppe practices in taxation, postal relays and diplomacy, took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tatars">Tatar</a> princes such as those of the Qasim Khanate into its service and set the pattern for later expansion into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volga">Volga</a> basin and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siberia">Siberia</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/vasily-iii-of-moscow"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/vasily-iii-of-moscow.jpg" alt="Vasily III of Moscow" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/vasily-iii-of-moscow">Vasily III of Moscow</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Vasili_III_of_Russia.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">André Thévet, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The grand duchy did not collapse but was transformed. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vasily-iii-of-moscow">Vasily III</a> (r. 1505–1533) completed his father's work, ending the independence of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pskov">Pskov</a> in 1510, taking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/smolensk">Smolensk</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lithuania">Lithuania</a> in 1514 and absorbing the last <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ryazan">Ryazan</a> principality around 1521, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> repeatedly intervened in the succession of the Khanate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kazan">Kazan</a>. By his death the state stretched from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/white-sea">White Sea</a> to the steppe frontier south of the Oka and from Smolensk towards the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ural-mountains">Urals</a>. His heir <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivan-iv-of-russia">Ivan IV</a> was three years old, and the regency of his mother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elena-glinskaya">Elena Glinskaya</a> (1533–1538) was followed by years of rivalry between boyar clans such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shuisky-family">Shuiskys</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belsky-family">Belskys</a>, which nonetheless left the institutions of the state intact. On 16 January 1547 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/macarius-metropolitan-of-moscow">Metropolitan Macarius</a> crowned the sixteen-year-old Ivan in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dormition-cathedral-moscow">Cathedral of the Dormition</a> as tsar, a title derived from Caesar that earlier rulers had used only occasionally. The coronation marked the end of the grand duchy as a political form and the beginning of the Tsardom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, which conquered Kazan in 1552 and Astrakhan in 1556, became the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peter-the-great">Peter the Great</a> in 1721 and passed on Moscow's autocratic traditions to the modern Russian state. Moscow lost its status as capital to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-petersburg">St Petersburg</a> in the early eighteenth century and regained it in 1918.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Grand Duchy of Moscow turned a small <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/appanage">appanage</a> into the core of the Russian state, using service to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-horde">Golden Horde</a>, the support of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox Church</a> and two centuries of annexations to end <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tatars">Tatar</a> overlordship in 1480 and lay the foundations of Russian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/autocracy">autocracy</a> before <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivan-iv-of-russia">Ivan IV</a> was crowned tsar in 1547.</em></p>

